from h34 import *
M=mcq;F=tf;S=short;N=num
b='<text x="10" y="22" font-weight="bold">Pairs: even numbers pair up, odd numbers leave one out</text>'
for n in range(1,11):
    x=20+(n-1)*66
    for k in range(n):
        cx=x+14+(k%2)*22; cy=50+(k//2)*22
        paired=(k< n-(n%2))
        b+=f'<circle cx="{cx}" cy="{cy}" r="8" fill="{"#3a9d5d" if paired else "#e4572e"}"/>'
    b+=f'<text x="{x+16}" y="{150+10}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">{n}</text><text x="{x+16}" y="{175}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">{"even" if n%2==0 else "odd"}</text>'
svgfile('c4-maths-even-odd.svg',700,190,b)
chapter(4,'maths','pattern-around-us','Pattern Around Us',
 ('Counting coconuts','Gundappa has some land with tall coconut trees, and Muniamma makes plates and cups from coconut shells. Shirley and Shiv love to arrange things in neat patterns. In this chapter you will count in smart ways, make patterns with play money, and find out why some numbers are called even and some are called odd.'),
 [('Counting in smart ways',['To count the coconut trees on Gundappa\'s land, you do not have to count one by one. You can count the trees in rows or in groups.','If each tree gives 5 coconuts, the total number of coconuts is 5 added once for every tree, which is repeated addition and also multiplication.','Muniamma stacks her plates and cups in neat piles, so we can count by looking at one pile and repeating it.','Coconut laddoos and milk pedas are arranged in trays that are exactly the same, and the trays are put one on top of the other. Count what is in one tray and multiply by the number of trays.','There are many ways to count the same things, and your way can be different from your friend\'s way. Share your way and listen to theirs.']),
  ('Patterns with money',['Shirley and Shiv arrange play money to make nice patterns. We have notes and coins of 1, 2, 5 and 10 rupees.','To count the money in a pattern, we can add 10s first, then 5s, then 2s and then 1s.','We can show 36 rupees with 3 tens, 1 five and 1 one. We can show 125 rupees with 12 tens and 1 five. We can show 183 rupees with 18 tens, 1 two and 1 one.','Ask a friend to read the amount from your arrangement. If your arrangement is neat, it is easy to read.']),
  ('Two ways to arrange: odd and even',['Shiv arranges his coins in pairs, two by two. When every coin has a partner and none is left alone, the number is an even number.','Shirley\'s arrangement always has one coin without a partner. Such a number is an odd number.','The even numbers are 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 and so on. The odd numbers are 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 and so on.','You can check by drawing the objects in pairs. Pair up the dots, and if one is left out, the number is odd.','All the numbers in the times 2 table are even, because we count them in twos.']),
  ('Even and odd up to 100',['The ones digit tells us whether a number is even or odd. Numbers ending in 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8 are even, and numbers ending in 1, 3, 5, 7 or 9 are odd.','So 30, 78 and 300 are even, and 415, 67 and 99 are odd. A bigger number like 154 is even, because it ends in 4.','There are as many even numbers as odd numbers between 1 and 100, because they take turns: odd, even, odd, even.','Shirley notices that the number before and the number after an odd number are both even. Shiv checks if the number before and after an even number are both odd. Try 4: its neighbours 3 and 5 are both odd. It works!','If you write any 10 numbers in order without skipping any, you will see odd and even numbers alternate again and again.','Look at the page numbers of your Maths book. As you turn the pages, the page numbers go odd, even, odd, even, so Shirley\'s pattern is easy to see.']),
  ('Making numbers with two digits',['With the digits 1 and 6, and no digit repeated, you can make 16 and 61. The number 16 is even and 61 is odd.','To make a number even, put an even digit in the ones place. To make it odd, put an odd digit in the ones place.','So if you choose the digits 3 and 8, you can make 38, which is even, and 83, which is odd.'])],
 [('Is 415 even or odd? How do you know?',[('Look at the ones digit of 415. It is 5.','The ones digit decides.'),('5 is one of the odd digits 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.','Numbers ending with an odd digit are odd.'),('So 415 is an odd number.','It cannot be paired up completely; one is always left out.')]),
  ('Show 36 rupees using notes and coins of 10, 5, 2 and 1 rupees.',[('Start with the biggest value. Three tens make 30 rupees.','10 + 10 + 10 = 30.'),('We still need 6 rupees. Take a 5 and a 1.','5 + 1 = 6.'),('30 + 5 + 1 = 36 rupees.','Check by adding all the parts.')]),
  ('Make two 2-digit numbers using the digits 2 and 7. Which is even and which is odd?',[('The two numbers are 27 and 72.','Use each digit once in each number.'),('27 ends in 7, which is odd.','Look at the ones digit.'),('72 ends in 2, which is even.','So 72 is even and 27 is odd.')])],
 ('Explain what even and odd numbers are, tell how to find them quickly, and show two ways of counting a group of coconuts.',['Even numbers pair up completely; odd numbers have one left out','Look at the ones digit: 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 are even','Numbers alternate odd and even','Group objects to count faster'],['A big number must be even','Only small numbers can be odd','You must count one by one']),
 ['We can count in groups, rows and repeated additions.','Money of 1, 2, 5 and 10 can be arranged to show any amount.','Even numbers can be paired; odd numbers have one left over.','A number is even if its ones digit is 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8; odd if it is 1, 3, 5, 7 or 9.','Odd and even numbers take turns on the number line.'],
 [M('1',1,'Which of these is an even number?','78',[('415','It ends in 5, so it is odd'),('67','It ends in 7, so it is odd')],'78 ends in 8, so it is even.'),
  M('2',1,'Which of these is an odd number?','99',[('300','It ends in 0, so it is even'),('154','It ends in 4, so it is even')],'99 ends in 9, so it is odd.'),
  F('3',1,'All the numbers in the times 2 table are even.',True,'We count in twos, so every number can be paired up completely.'),
  F('4',1,'A number is odd if it can be put in pairs with none left out.',False,'That describes an even number. An odd number always has one left without a partner.'),
  N('5',2,'How many rupees are three 10-rupee notes, one 5-rupee coin and one 1-rupee coin?',36,'30 + 5 + 1 = 36 rupees.',[(35,'You forgot the 1-rupee coin'),(16,'You added 10 + 5 + 1 only once')],'rupees'),
  M('6',2,'What are the 2-digit numbers that can be made with the digits 1 and 6?','16 and 61',[('11 and 66','Digits cannot be repeated'),('16 and 16','They are the same number twice')],'Use each digit once: 16 and 61.'),
  M('7',2,'Which of the numbers 16 and 61 is even?','16',[('61','61 ends in 1, so it is odd'),('Both','Only one of them ends in an even digit')],'16 ends in 6, so it is even.'),
  M('8',2,'The number before and the number after an odd number are always...','even',[('odd','Odd numbers are two steps apart'),('zero','Not zero')],'If you take 7, then 6 and 8 are both even.'),
  M('9',2,'If you list consecutive numbers 20, 21, 22, 23, what pattern do you see?','Even, odd, even, odd',[('All even','21 and 23 are odd'),('All odd','20 and 22 are even')],'Even and odd numbers take turns.'),
  S('10',3,'What do we call numbers that can be arranged in pairs with none left out?',['even','even numbers','even number'],'Even numbers pair up completely.'),
  M('11',3,'Which digit in the ones place makes a number odd?','7',[('0','Numbers ending in 0 are even'),('8','Numbers ending in 8 are even')],'Odd numbers end in 1, 3, 5, 7 or 9.'),
  M('12',3,'Which of these is true for numbers between 1 and 100?','There are as many even numbers as odd numbers',[('There are more even numbers','They alternate, so the counts are equal'),('There are more odd numbers','They alternate, so the counts are equal')],'Even and odd numbers take turns, 50 of each.')],
 svgs=[('c4-maths-even-odd','Groups of dots for the numbers 1 to 10, with green paired dots and one orange left-out dot for each odd number.','Even numbers pair up completely; odd numbers leave one out.')],
 keep=['even','odd'])
